```bash mkdir my-api && cd my-api npm init -y npm install express dotenv npm install -D nodemon ```

Entry point with the basics — JSON parsing, environment config, and a health check (your future monitoring will thank you):

```js import express from 'express'; import 'dotenv/config';

const app = express(); app.use(express.json());

app.get('/health', (req, res) => res.json({ status: 'ok' }));

const PORT = process.env.PORT || 3000; app.listen(PORT, () => console.log(`Listening on ${PORT}`)); ```

Use ES modules (`"type": "module"` in package.json) — it's the modern default and what most libraries now assume.

Every request body, query parameter, and URL parameter is hostile until proven otherwise. Validate with a schema library — Zod is the current standard:

```js import { z } from 'zod';

const createUserSchema = z.object({ name: z.string().min(1).max(100), email: z.string().email(), age: z.number().int().min(13).optional(), });

app.post('/users', (req, res, next) => { const result = createUserSchema.safeParse(req.body); if (!result.success) { return res.status(400).json({ error: 'Invalid input', details: result.error.flatten(), }); } // result.data is now typed and safe }); ```

Validate at the boundary (middleware or the top of the controller) so service code can assume clean input. This one practice eliminates an entire class of bugs and most injection-style vulnerabilities.

Ad-hoc error responses (`res.status(500).send('oops')` in some places, JSON in others) make client code miserable. Centralize with an error-handling middleware — Express routes errors there when you call `next(err)`:

```js // Custom error classes class NotFoundError extends Error { status = 404; } class ValidationError extends Error { status = 400; }

// In a controller: if (!user) throw new NotFoundError('User not found');

// Central handler (registered LAST, after all routes): app.use((err, req, res, next) => { const status = err.status || 500; if (status === 500) console.error(err); // log server errors with stack res.status(status).json({ error: status === 500 ? 'Internal server error' : err.message, }); }); ```

Note: async route handlers need errors forwarded — either wrap handlers in a helper that catches promise rejections into `next()`, or use Express 5, which handles async errors natively. Unhandled promise rejections crashing the process is a classic production incident; eliminate the possibility structurally.

For most APIs, JSON Web Tokens are the pragmatic choice: the server signs a token at login, the client sends it in the `Authorization: Bearer <token>` header, and middleware verifies it per request.

```js import jwt from 'jsonwebtoken';

function authenticate(req, res, next) { const header = req.headers.authorization; const token = header?.startsWith('Bearer ') ? header.slice(7) : null; if (!token) return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Missing token' }); try { req.user = jwt.verify(token, process.env.JWT_SECRET); next(); } catch { return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Invalid or expired token' }); } }

app.get('/profile', authenticate, (req, res) => { res.json({ userId: req.user.sub }); }); ```

Keep tokens short-lived (15 minutes to a few hours) and use refresh tokens for long sessions. Store the JWT secret in environment variables, rotate it if it's ever exposed, and never put sensitive data in the token payload — it's signed, not encrypted, and anyone can decode it.
